{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12593","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12593","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12593","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yimin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mario","familyName":"de Sousa","name":"de Sousa, Mario","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Converting IEC 61131-3 LD into SFC Using Large Language Model: Dataset and Testing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T02:39:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-06T02:00:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/etfa65518.2025.11205542","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In the domain of Programmable Logic Controller (PLC) programming, converting a Ladder Diagram (LD) into a Sequential Function Chart (SFC) is an inherently challenging problem, primarily due to the lack of domain-specific knowledge and the issue of state explosion in existing algorithms. However, the rapid development of Artificial Intelligence (AI) - especially Large Language Model (LLM) - offers a promising new approach.\n Despite this potential, data-driven approaches in this field have been hindered by a lack of suitable datasets. To address this gap, we constructed several datasets consisting of paired textual representations of SFC and LD programs that conform to the IEC 61131-3 standard.\n Based on these datasets, we explored the feasibility of automating the LD-SFC conversion using LLM. Our preliminary experiments show that a fine-tuned LLM model achieves up to 91% accuracy on certain dataset, with the lowest observed accuracy being 79%, suggesting that with proper training and representation, LLMs can effectively support LD-SFC conversion. These early results highlight the viability and future potential of this approach."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12593","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:44:00Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:44:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-06T03:15:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13145","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13145","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13145","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yudong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yudong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaokang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaokang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaofeng","familyName":"zhao","name":"zhao, Shaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongfeng","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Rongfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UTI-LLM: A Personalized Articulatory-Speech Therapy Assistance System Based on Multimodal Large Language Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T15:02:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:51:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-31T06:11:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-03T01:28:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speech therapy is essential for rehabilitating speech disorders caused by neurological impairments such as stroke. However, traditional manual and computer-assisted systems are limited in real-time accessibility and articulatory motion feedback. Recent advances in multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have demonstrated significant potential in healthcare, especially through their adaptive assessment and therapeutic feedback capabilities. Nevertheless, challenges including insufficient acquisition and fusion of articulatory information, inadequate parsing of articulatory organ motion trajectories, and the scarcity of domain-specific datasets hinder the application of MLLMs in speech therapy. To address these limitations, we propose an MLLM-based speech rehabilitation assistance system that leverages ultrasound tongue imaging and speech signals to deliver precise, interactive articulatory feedback. We construct a high-quality domain-specific dataset comprising ultrasound-speech dialogue pairs. This dataset facilitates fine-tuning to enhance the model's clinical adaptability. Furthermore, our method develops spatiotemporal fusion training strategy of ultrasound videos and speech signals, enabling fine-grained articulatory impairment analysis and ultimately generating actionable feedback. Experimental results demonstrate the effectiveness of our model in articulatory analysis and clinical assessment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13145","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:56:57Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:56:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-03T03:00:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13031","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13031","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13031","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Long","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teng","familyName":"Xi","name":"Xi, Teng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Long","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingdong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jingdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Perception Before Reasoning: Two-Stage Reinforcement Learning for Visual Reasoning in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T12:51:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:45:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-17T10:09:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-20T00:37:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has proven highly effective in eliciting the reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs). Inspired by this success, recent studies have explored applying similar techniques to vision-language models (VLMs), aiming to enhance their reasoning performance. However, directly transplanting RL methods from LLMs to VLMs is suboptimal, as the tasks faced by VLMs are inherently more complex. Specifically, VLMs must first accurately perceive and understand visual inputs before reasoning can be effectively performed. To address this challenge, we propose a two-stage reinforcement learning framework designed to jointly enhance both the perceptual and reasoning capabilities of VLMs. To mitigate the vanishing advantage issue commonly observed in RL training, we first perform dataset-level sampling to selectively strengthen specific capabilities using distinct data sources. During training, the first stage focuses on improving the model's visual perception through coarse- and fine-grained visual understanding, while the second stage targets the enhancement of reasoning abilities. After the proposed two-stage reinforcement learning process, we obtain PeBR-R1, a vision-language model with significantly enhanced perceptual and reasoning capabilities. Experimental results on seven benchmark datasets demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach and validate the superior performance of PeBR-R1 across diverse visual reasoning tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13031","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:54:13Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:54:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-20T02:01:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12574","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12574","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12574","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyuan","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Siyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiguang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhiguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanzhou","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Hanzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinpeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xinpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Yet Another Watermark for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T02:04:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:15:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T03:13:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-17T00:18:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing watermarking methods for large language models (LLMs) mainly embed watermark by adjusting the token sampling prediction or post-processing, lacking intrinsic coupling with LLMs, which may significantly reduce the semantic quality of the generated marked texts. Traditional watermarking methods based on training or fine-tuning may be extendable to LLMs. However, most of them are limited to the white-box scenario, or very time-consuming due to the massive parameters of LLMs. In this paper, we present a new watermarking framework for LLMs, where the watermark is embedded into the LLM by manipulating the internal parameters of the LLM, and can be extracted from the generated text without accessing the LLM. Comparing with related methods, the proposed method entangles the watermark with the intrinsic parameters of the LLM, which better balances the robustness and imperceptibility of the watermark. Moreover, the proposed method enables us to extract the watermark under the black-box scenario, which is computationally efficient for use. Experimental results have also verified the feasibility, superiority and practicality. This work provides a new perspective different from mainstream works, which may shed light on future research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"https://scholar.google.com/citations?hl=en\u0026amp;user=IdiF7M0AAAAJ"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12574","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:43:33Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:43:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-17T04:11:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12384","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12384","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12384","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seth","familyName":"Ockerman","name":"Ockerman, Seth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amal","familyName":"Gueroudji","name":"Gueroudji, Amal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Song Young","familyName":"Oh","name":"Oh, Song Young","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Underwood","name":"Underwood, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Chia","name":"Chia, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyle","familyName":"Chard","name":"Chard, Kyle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Ross","name":"Ross, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shivaram","familyName":"Venkataraman","name":"Venkataraman, Shivaram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring Distributed Vector Databases Performance on HPC Platforms: A Study with Qdrant"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T19:25:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:05:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-16T15:46:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-17T01:02:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vector databases have rapidly grown in popularity, enabling efficient similarity search over data such as text, images, and video. They now play a central role in modern AI workflows, aiding large language models by grounding model outputs in external literature through retrieval-augmented generation. Despite their importance, little is known about the performance characteristics of vector databases in high-performance computing (HPC) systems that drive large-scale science. This work presents an empirical study of distributed vector database performance on the Polaris supercomputer in the Argonne Leadership Computing Facility. We construct a realistic biological-text workload from BV-BRC and generate embeddings from the peS2o corpus using Qwen3-Embedding-4B. We select Qdrant to evaluate insertion, index construction, and query latency with up to 32 workers. Informed by practical lessons from our experience, this work takes a first step toward characterizing vector database performance on HPC platforms to guide future research and optimization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in the SC'25 Workshop Frontiers in Generative AI for HPC Science and Engineering: Foundations, Challenges, and Opportunities"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12384","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:39:12Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:39:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-17T04:11:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13312","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13312","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13312","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijian","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shen","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Shen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Houquan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Houquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaopeng","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Shaopeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengjun","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Pengjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingren","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jingren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WebWeaver: Structuring Web-Scale Evidence with Dynamic Outlines for Open-Ended Deep Research"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:57:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:59:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-29T14:27:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-30T02:06:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T11:47:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:42:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper tackles \\textbf{open-ended deep research (OEDR)}, a complex challenge where AI agents must synthesize vast web-scale information into insightful reports. Current approaches are plagued by dual-fold limitations: static research pipelines that decouple planning from evidence acquisition and monolithic generation paradigms that include redundant, irrelevant evidence, suffering from hallucination issues and low citation accuracy. To address these challenges, we introduce \\textbf{WebWeaver}, a novel dual-agent framework that emulates the human research process. The planner operates in a dynamic cycle, iteratively interleaving evidence acquisition with outline optimization to produce a comprehensive, citation-grounded outline linking to a memory bank of evidence. The writer then executes a hierarchical retrieval and writing process, composing the report section by section. By performing targeted retrieval of only the necessary evidence from the memory bank via citations for each part, it effectively mitigates long-context issues and citation hallucinations. Our framework establishes a new state-of-the-art across major OEDR benchmarks, including DeepResearch Bench, DeepConsult, and DeepResearchGym. These results validate our human-centric, iterative methodology, demonstrating that adaptive planning and focused synthesis are crucial for producing comprehensive, trusted, and well-structured reports."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"An agent system for open-ended deep research"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13312","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T02:00:54Z","registered":"2025-09-17T02:00:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:24:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13307","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13307","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13307","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toby","familyName":"Lovick","name":"Lovick, Toby","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Yallup","name":"Yallup, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davide","familyName":"Piras","name":"Piras, Davide","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessio Spurio","familyName":"Mancini","name":"Mancini, Alessio Spurio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Will","familyName":"Handley","name":"Handley, Will","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"High-Dimensional Bayesian Model Comparison in Cosmology with GPU-accelerated Nested Sampling and Neural Emulators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:57:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:59:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-06T20:37:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:09:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We demonstrate a GPU-accelerated nested sampling framework for efficient high-dimensional Bayesian inference in cosmology. Using JAX-based neural emulators and likelihoods for cosmic microwave background and cosmic shear analyses, our approach provides parameter constraints and direct calculation of Bayesian evidence. In the 39-dimensional $Λ$CDM vs $w_0w_a$ shear analysis, we produce Bayes factors and a robust error bar in just 2 days on a single A100 GPU, without loss of accuracy. Where CPU-based nested sampling can now be outpaced by methods relying on MCMC sampling and decoupled evidence estimation, we demonstrate that with GPU acceleration nested sampling offers the necessary speed-up to put it on equal computational footing with these methods, especially where reliable model comparison is paramount. We also explore interpolation in the matter power spectrum for cosmic shear analysis, finding a further factor of 4 speed-up with consistent posterior contours and Bayes factor. We put forward both nested and gradient-based sampling as useful tools for the modern cosmologist, where cutting-edge inference pipelines can yield orders of magnitude improvements in computation time."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages 5 figures. Updated to add clarity in the main results table and detail added throughout. Comments welcome"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13307","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T02:00:47Z","registered":"2025-09-17T02:00:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:21:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13046","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13046","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13046","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eyal","familyName":"German","name":"German, Eyal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Samira","name":"Samira, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuval","familyName":"Elovici","name":"Elovici, Yuval","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asaf","familyName":"Shabtai","name":"Shabtai, Asaf","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MIA-EPT: Membership Inference Attack via Error Prediction for Tabular Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T13:03:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:45:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-05T07:26:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:50:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Synthetic data generation plays an important role in enabling data sharing, particularly in sensitive domains like healthcare and finance. Recent advances in diffusion models have made it possible to generate realistic, high-quality tabular data, but they may also memorize training records and leak sensitive information. Membership inference attacks (MIAs) exploit this vulnerability by determining whether a record was used in training. While MIAs have been studied in images and text, their use against tabular diffusion models remains underexplored despite the unique risks of structured attributes and limited record diversity. In this paper, we introduce MIAEPT, Membership Inference Attack via Error Prediction for Tabular Data, a novel black-box attack specifically designed to target tabular diffusion models. MIA-EPT constructs errorbased feature vectors by masking and reconstructing attributes of target records, disclosing membership signals based on how well these attributes are predicted. MIA-EPT operates without access to the internal components of the generative model, relying only on its synthetic data output, and was shown to generalize across multiple state-of-the-art diffusion models. We validate MIA-EPT on three diffusion-based synthesizers, achieving AUC-ROC scores of up to 0.599 and TPR@10% FPR values of 22.0% in our internal tests. Under the MIDST 2025 competition conditions, MIA-EPT achieved second place in the Black-box Multi-Table track (TPR@10% FPR = 20.0%). These results demonstrate that our method can uncover substantial membership leakage in synthetic tabular data, challenging the assumption that synthetic data is inherently privacy-preserving. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/eyalgerman/MIA-EPT."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13046","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:54:34Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:54:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:48:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12990","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12990","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12990","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyu","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Boyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianqian","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Qianqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shilong","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Shilong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhiyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sicong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Sicong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingming","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Qingming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dual-Stage Reweighted MoE for Long-Tailed Egocentric Mistake Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T12:00:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:42:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T15:18:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-06T00:45:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this report, we address the problem of determining whether a user performs an action incorrectly from egocentric video data. To handle the challenges posed by subtle and infrequent mistakes, we propose a Dual-Stage Reweighted Mixture-of-Experts (DR-MoE) framework. In the first stage, features are extracted using a frozen ViViT model and a LoRA-tuned ViViT model, which are combined through a feature-level expert module. In the second stage, three classifiers are trained with different objectives: reweighted cross-entropy to mitigate class imbalance, AUC loss to improve ranking under skewed distributions, and label-aware loss with sharpness-aware minimization to enhance calibration and generalization. Their predictions are fused using a classification-level expert module. The proposed method achieves strong performance, particularly in identifying rare and ambiguous mistake instances. The code is available at https://github.com/boyuh/DR-MoE."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12990","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:53:16Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:53:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-06T02:04:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12960","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12960","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12960","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuval","familyName":"Weiss","name":"Weiss, Yuval","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David Demitri","familyName":"Africa","name":"Africa, David Demitri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paula","familyName":"Buttery","name":"Buttery, Paula","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard Diehl","familyName":"Martinez","name":"Martinez, Richard Diehl","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Investigating ReLoRA: Effects on the Learning Dynamics of Small Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T11:06:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:40:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T14:09:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:49:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Parameter-efficient methods like LoRA have revolutionised large language model (LLM) fine-tuning. ReLoRA extends this idea to pretraining by repeatedly merging and reinitialising low-rank adapters, increasing cumulative rank while keeping updates cheap. This aligns well with observations that high-capacity models learn through locally low-rank trajectories that expand over time. By contrast, recent work suggests that small language models (SLMs) exhibit rank deficiencies and under-utilise their available dimensionality. This raises a natural question: can ReLoRA's rank-expanding update rule \\textit{steer} SLMs toward healthier learning dynamics, mitigating rank bottlenecks in a capacity-constrained regime? We argue SLMs are an ideal testbed: they train quickly, enable controlled ablations, and make rank phenomena more measurable. We present the first systematic study of ReLoRA in SLMs (11M-66M parameters), evaluating both performance and learning dynamics. Across loss, Paloma perplexity, and BLiMP, we find that ReLoRA underperforms full-rank training, with gaps widening at larger scales. Analysis of proportional effective rank and condition numbers shows that ReLoRA amplifies existing rank deficiencies and induces ill-conditioned updates early in training. Our results suggest that while ReLoRA's merge-and-restart strategy can expand ranks in larger models, it does not straightforwardly translate to capacity-limited SLMs, motivating adaptive-rank or hybrid-rank approaches for low-compute pretraining."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 Pages, 6 Tables, 8 Figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12960","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:52:35Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:52:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:36:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13198","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13198","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13198","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thành","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Thành","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Teytelboym","name":"Teytelboym, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shai","familyName":"Vardi","name":"Vardi, Shai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficiency, Envy, and Incentives in Combinatorial Assignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Theoretical Economics (econ.TH)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T16:01:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:55:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:28:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:17:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Ensuring efficiency and envy-freeness in allocating indivisible goods without money often requires randomization. However, existing combinatorial assignment mechanisms (for applications such as course allocation, food banks, and refugee resettlement) guarantee these properties either ex ante or ex post, but not both. We propose a new class of mechanisms based on Competitive Equilibrium from Random Incomes (CERI): Agents receive random token budgets and select optimal lotteries at competitive prices that clear markets in expectation. Our main insight is to let the CERI price vector guide all ex-post allocations. We show that all ordinally efficient allocations are CERI allocations, which can be implemented as lotteries over near-feasible Pareto-efficient outcomes. With identical budget distributions, CERI allocations are ordinally envy-free; with budget distributions on small supports, ex-post allocations are envy-free up to one good. Moreover, we design an asymptotically efficient implementation of CERI that satisfies a strong new non-manipulability property in large markets."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Abstract published in EC'25 (https://dl.acm.org/doi/abs/10.1145/3736252.3742573)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13198","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:58:12Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:58:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:29:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12838","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12838","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12838","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kento","familyName":"Murata","name":"Murata, Kento","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shoichi","familyName":"Hasegawa","name":"Hasegawa, Shoichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomochika","familyName":"Ishikawa","name":"Ishikawa, Tomochika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoshinobu","familyName":"Hagiwara","name":"Hagiwara, Yoshinobu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akira","familyName":"Taniguchi","name":"Taniguchi, Akira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lotfi El","familyName":"Hafi","name":"Hafi, Lotfi El","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tadahiro","familyName":"Taniguchi","name":"Taniguchi, Tadahiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Robot Task Planning for Multi-Object Retrieval Tasks with Distributed On-Site Knowledge via Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T09:00:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:34:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-30T12:31:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-01T00:59:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"It is crucial to efficiently execute instructions such as \"Find an apple and a banana\" or \"Get ready for a field trip,\" which require searching for multiple objects or understanding context-dependent commands. This study addresses the challenging problem of determining which robot should be assigned to which part of a task when each robot possesses different situational on-site knowledge-specifically, spatial concepts learned from the area designated to it by the user. We propose a task planning framework that leverages large language models (LLMs) and spatial concepts to decompose natural language instructions into subtasks and allocate them to multiple robots. We designed a novel few-shot prompting strategy that enables LLMs to infer required objects from ambiguous commands and decompose them into appropriate subtasks. In our experiments, the proposed method achieved 47/50 successful assignments, outperforming random (28/50) and commonsense-based assignment (26/50). Furthermore, we conducted qualitative evaluations using two actual mobile manipulators. The results demonstrated that our framework could handle instructions, including those involving ad hoc categories such as \"Get ready for a field trip,\" by successfully performing task decomposition, assignment, sequential planning, and execution."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to AROB-ISBC 2026 (Journal Track option)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12838","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:49:44Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:49:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-01T03:04:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12227","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12227","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12227","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marzieh","familyName":"Ajirak","name":"Ajirak, Marzieh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oded","familyName":"Bein","name":"Bein, Oded","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ellen Rose","familyName":"Bowen","name":"Bowen, Ellen Rose","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dora","familyName":"Kanellopoulos","name":"Kanellopoulos, Dora","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avital","familyName":"Falk","name":"Falk, Avital","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faith M.","familyName":"Gunning","name":"Gunning, Faith M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nili","familyName":"Solomonov","name":"Solomonov, Nili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Logan","familyName":"Grosenick","name":"Grosenick, Logan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning to Route: Per-Sample Adaptive Routing for Multimodal Multitask Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-06T16:49:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:00:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-29T15:42:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-30T02:12:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a unified framework for adaptive routing in multitask, multimodal prediction settings where data heterogeneity and task interactions vary across samples. Motivated by applications in psychotherapy where structured assessments and unstructured clinician notes coexist with partially missing data and correlated outcomes, we introduce a routing-based architecture that dynamically selects modality processing pathways and task-sharing strategies on a per-sample basis. Our model defines multiple modality paths, including raw and fused representations of text and numeric features and learns to route each input through the most informative expert combination. Task-specific predictions are produced by shared or independent heads depending on the routing decision, and the entire system is trained end-to-end. We evaluate the model on both synthetic data and real-world psychotherapy notes predicting depression and anxiety outcomes. Our experiments show that our method consistently outperforms fixed multitask or single-task baselines, and that the learned routing policy provides interpretable insights into modality relevance and task structure. This addresses critical challenges in personalized healthcare by enabling per-subject adaptive information processing that accounts for data heterogeneity and task correlations. Applied to psychotherapy, this framework could improve mental health outcomes, enhance treatment assignment precision, and increase clinical cost-effectiveness through personalized intervention strategies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12227","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:35:37Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:35:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-30T05:21:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13067","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13067","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13067","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiaolei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotian","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Haotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyang","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Xiangyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T13:22:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:46:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-29T07:44:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-30T01:41:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"By cropping high-resolution images into local tiles and encoding them independently, High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models (HR-LVLMs) have demonstrated remarkable fine-grained visual understanding capabilities. However, this divide-and-conquer paradigm significantly increases the number of visual tokens, resulting in substantial computational and memory overhead. To better understand and address this challenge, we empirically investigate visual token utilization in HR-LVLMs and uncover three key findings: (1) the local tiles have varying importance, jointly determined by visual saliency and task relevance; (2) the CLS token in CLIP-based vision encoders exhibits a two-stage attention pattern across layers, with each stage attending to different types of visual tokens; (3) the visual tokens emphasized at different stages encode information at varying levels of granularity, playing complementary roles within LVLMs. Building on these insights, we propose HERO, a High-resolution visual token early dropping framework that integrates content-adaptive token budget allocation with function-aware token selection. By accurately estimating tile-level importance and selectively retaining visual tokens with complementary roles, HERO achieves superior efficiency-accuracy trade-offs across diverse benchmarks and model scales, all in a training-free manner. This study provides both empirical insights and practical solutions toward efficient inference in HR-LVLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13067","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:55:02Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:55:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-30T05:19:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12307","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12307","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12307","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuli","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Xuli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Poonam","familyName":"Lohan","name":"Lohan, Poonam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Burak","familyName":"Kantarci","name":"Kantarci, Burak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FLARE: Flying Learning Agents for Resource Efficiency in Next-Gen UAV Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T18:00:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-30T00:20:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/lnet.2025.3611236","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This letter addresses a critical challenge in the context of 6G and beyond wireless networks, the joint optimization of power and bandwidth resource allocation for aerial intelligent platforms, specifically uncrewed aerial vehicles (UAVs), operating in highly dynamic environments with mobile ground user equipment (UEs). We introduce FLARE (Flying Learning Agents for Resource Efficiency), a learning-enabled aerial intelligence framework that jointly optimizes UAV positioning, altitude, transmit power, and bandwidth allocation in real-time. To adapt to UE mobility, we employ Silhouette-based K-Means clustering, enabling dynamic grouping of users and UAVs' deployment at cluster centroids for efficient service delivery. The problem is modeled as a multi-agent control task, with bandwidth discretized into resource blocks and power treated as a continuous variable. To solve this, our proposed framework, FLARE, employs a hybrid reinforcement learning strategy that combines Multi-Agent Deep Deterministic Policy Gradient (MADDPG) and Deep Q-Network (DQN) to enhance learning efficiency. Simulation results demonstrate that our method significantly enhances user coverage, achieving a 73.45% improvement in the number of served users under a 5 Mbps data rate constraint, outperforming MADDPG baseline."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 7 figures, accepted to IEEE Networking Letters"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12307","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:37:27Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:37:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-30T05:09:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12446","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12446","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12446","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dawei","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Dawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenyan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Wenyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kexin","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Kexin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianqi","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Tianqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixu","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Zixu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianchang","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Jianchang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PromptSculptor: Multi-Agent Based Text-to-Image Prompt Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T20:52:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:08:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T03:51:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-25T00:23:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid advancement of generative AI has democratized access to powerful tools such as Text-to-Image models. However, to generate high-quality images, users must still craft detailed prompts specifying scene, style, and context-often through multiple rounds of refinement. We propose PromptSculptor, a novel multi-agent framework that automates this iterative prompt optimization process. Our system decomposes the task into four specialized agents that work collaboratively to transform a short, vague user prompt into a comprehensive, refined prompt. By leveraging Chain-of-Thought reasoning, our framework effectively infers hidden context and enriches scene and background details. To iteratively refine the prompt, a self-evaluation agent aligns the modified prompt with the original input, while a feedback-tuning agent incorporates user feedback for further refinement. Experimental results demonstrate that PromptSculptor significantly enhances output quality and reduces the number of iterations needed for user satisfaction. Moreover, its model-agnostic design allows seamless integration with various T2I models, paving the way for industrial applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 System Demonstration Track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12446","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:40:37Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:40:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-25T02:25:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13232","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13232","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13232","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongwen","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhongwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Single-stream Policy Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T16:39:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:56:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T14:19:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:56:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We revisit policy-gradient optimization for Large Language Models (LLMs) from a single-stream perspective. Prevailing group-based methods like GRPO reduce variance with on-the-fly baselines but suffer from critical flaws: frequent degenerate groups erase learning signals, and synchronization barriers hinder scalability. We introduce Single-stream Policy Optimization (SPO), which eliminates these issues by design. SPO replaces per-group baselines with a persistent, KL-adaptive value tracker and normalizes advantages globally across the batch, providing a stable, low-variance learning signal for every sample. Being group-free, SPO enables higher throughput and scales effectively in long-horizon or tool-integrated settings where generation times vary. Furthermore, the persistent value tracker naturally enables an adaptive curriculum via prioritized sampling. Experiments using Qwen3-8B show that SPO converges more smoothly and attains higher accuracy than GRPO, while eliminating computation wasted on degenerate groups. Ablation studies confirm that SPO's gains stem from its principled approach to baseline estimation and advantage normalization, offering a more robust and efficient path for LLM reasoning. Across five hard math benchmarks with Qwen3 8B, SPO improves the average maj@32 by +3.4 percentage points (pp) over GRPO, driven by substantial absolute point gains on challenging datasets, including +7.3 pp on BRUMO 25, +4.4 pp on AIME 25, +3.3 pp on HMMT 25, and achieves consistent relative gain in pass@$k$ across the evaluated $k$ values. SPO's success challenges the prevailing trend of adding incidental complexity to RL algorithms, highlighting a path where fundamental principles, not architectural workarounds, drive the next wave of progress in LLM reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13232","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:58:58Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:58:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-24T02:38:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13113","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13113","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13113","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongfang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Dongfang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yumin","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Yumin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengkai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shengkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spatiotemporally Resolved Measurements of CO2 Distribution at the Air-Water Interface Using Tunable Diode Laser Spectroscopy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T14:14:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T14:24:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-21T16:25:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:58:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The transport of CO2 across the air-water interface is central to physical oceanography and carbon sequestration. A comprehensive understanding of this process requires high-resolution diagnostics of diffusion, absorption, and reaction across a wide range of spatial and temporal scales. The current study presents a novel measurement method to quantify the CO2 distribution at the air-water interface. This method combines the advantages of tunable diode laser spectroscopy and rapid spatial beam scanning for in situ, nonintrusive, and spatiotemporally resolved measurement of the CO2 concentration distribution above the interface. The performance of this method was examined in a series of quasi-1D experiments in a miniature gas chamber, where the diffusion and absorption of CO2 into pure water and alkaline solutions of different pH values were continuously monitored. An effective time resolution of 5 ms and an effective spatial resolution of 1 mm were achieved. The observed gas-phase CO2 distribution evolution agreed with the classic one-dimensional diffusion model, which validated the accuracy of the current method. PH-dependent dynamics of interfacial CO2 concentration were also observed. The CO2 depletion rate is highly pH-sensitive at low pH and saturates at pH = 10, revealing complex competition between the gas-phase and the liquid-phase transport processes. The current method's high spatial and temporal resolution holds promise for studying cross-interface gas transport under more complex flow conditions, in both field measurements and laboratory studies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13113","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:56:13Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:56:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:56:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12594","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12594","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12594","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Titong","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Titong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuefeng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Xuefeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bailin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bailin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yahui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yahui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianpeng","familyName":"Lang","name":"Lang, Xianpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Better You Learn, The Smarter You Prune: Towards Efficient Vision-language-action Models via Differentiable Token Pruning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T02:43:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:17:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-21T13:51:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:55:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present LightVLA, a simple yet effective differentiable token pruning framework for vision-language-action (VLA) models. While VLA models have shown impressive capability in executing real-world robotic tasks, their deployment on resource-constrained platforms is often bottlenecked by the heavy attention-based computation over large sets of visual tokens. LightVLA addresses this challenge through adaptive, performance-driven pruning of visual tokens: It generates dynamic queries to evaluate visual token importance, and adopts Gumbel softmax to enable differentiable token selection. Through fine-tuning, LightVLA learns to preserve the most informative visual tokens while pruning tokens which do not contribute to task execution, thereby improving efficiency and performance simultaneously. Notably, LightVLA requires no heuristic magic numbers and introduces no additional trainable parameters, making it compatible with modern inference frameworks. Experimental results demonstrate that LightVLA outperforms different VLA models and existing token pruning methods across diverse tasks on the LIBERO benchmark, achieving higher success rates with substantially reduced computational overhead. Specifically, LightVLA reduces FLOPs and latency by 59.1% and 38.2% respectively, with a 2.6% improvement in task success rate. Meanwhile, we also investigate the learnable query-based token pruning method LightVLA* with additional trainable parameters, which also achieves satisfactory performance. Our work reveals that as VLA pursues optimal performance, LightVLA spontaneously learns to prune tokens from a performance-driven perspective. To the best of our knowledge, LightVLA is the first work to apply adaptive visual token pruning to VLA tasks with the collateral goals of efficiency and performance, marking a significant step toward more efficient, powerful and practical real-time robotic systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under review. Project site: https://liauto-research.github.io/LightVLA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12594","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:44:01Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:44:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:56:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13309","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13309","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13309","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zile","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Zile","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoxin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guoxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanzhong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xuanzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donglei","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Donglei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbiao","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Wenbiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baixuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Baixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huifeng","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Huifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Min","name":"Min, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minpeng","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Minpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengjun","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Pengjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingren","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jingren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WebResearcher: Unleashing unbounded reasoning capability in Long-Horizon Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:57:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:59:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-20T14:27:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:32:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in deep-research systems have demonstrated the potential for AI agents to autonomously discover and synthesize knowledge from external sources. In this paper, we introduce WebResearcher, a novel framework for building such agents through two key components: (1) WebResearcher, an iterative deep-research paradigm that reformulates deep research as a Markov Decision Process, where agents periodically consolidate findings into evolving reports while maintaining focused workspaces, overcoming the context suffocation and noise contamination that plague existing mono-contextual approaches; and (2) WebFrontier, a scalable data synthesis engine that generates high-quality training data through tool-augmented complexity escalation, enabling systematic creation of research tasks that bridge the gap between passive knowledge recall and active knowledge construction. Notably, we find that the training data from our paradigm significantly enhances tool-use capabilities even for traditional mono-contextual methods. Furthermore, our paradigm naturally scales through parallel thinking, enabling concurrent multi-agent exploration for more comprehensive conclusions. Extensive experiments across 6 challenging benchmarks demonstrate that WebResearcher achieves state-of-the-art performance, even surpassing frontier proprietary systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"https://tongyi-agent.github.io/blog/introducing-tongyi-deep-research/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13309","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T02:00:50Z","registered":"2025-09-17T02:00:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:56:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12385","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12385","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12385","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mitchell","familyName":"Plyler","name":"Plyler, Mitchell","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yilun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Tuzhilin","name":"Tuzhilin, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saoud","familyName":"Khalifah","name":"Khalifah, Saoud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sen","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Sen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SENTRA: Selected-Next-Token Transformer for LLM Text Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T19:26:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:05:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-19T15:48:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-22T00:54:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLMs are becoming increasingly capable and widespread. Consequently, the potential and reality of their misuse is also growing. In this work, we address the problem of detecting LLM-generated text that is not explicitly declared as such. We present a novel, general-purpose, and supervised LLM text detector, SElected-Next-Token tRAnsformer (SENTRA). SENTRA is a Transformer-based encoder leveraging selected-next-token-probability sequences and utilizing contrastive pre-training on large amounts of unlabeled data. Our experiments on three popular public datasets across 24 domains of text demonstrate SENTRA is a general-purpose classifier that significantly outperforms popular baselines in the out-of-domain setting."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP Findings 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12385","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:39:14Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:39:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-22T02:48:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12832","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12832","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12832","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atta ur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Atta ur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M. Y.","familyName":"Abd-Rabbou","name":"Abd-Rabbou, M. Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cong-feng","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Cong-feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Quantum Control Hierarchy: When Physics-Informed Design Meets Machine Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T08:56:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:34:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-19T09:52:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-22T00:37:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We address a wide spectrum of quantum control strategies, including various open-loop protocols and advanced adaptive methods. These methodologies apply to few-qubit scenarios and naturally scale to larger N-qubit systems. We benchmark them across fundamental quantum tasks: entanglement preservation/generation, and directed quantum transport in a disordered quantum walk. All simulations are performed in a challenging environment featuring non-Markov colored noise, imperfections, and the Markov Lindblad equation. With a complex task-dependent performance hierarchy, our deterministic protocols proved highly effective for entanglement generation/preservation, and in specific pulse configurations, they even outperformed the RL-optimization. In contrast, more advanced methods demonstrate a marked specialization. For entanglement preservation, a physics-informed hybrid Quantum Error Correction and Dynamical Decoupling (QEC-DD) protocol provides the most stable and effective solution, outperforming all other approaches. Conversely, for dynamic tasks requiring the discovery of non-trivial control sequences, such as DD, Floquet engineering, and rapid entanglement generation or coherent transport, the model-free Reinforcement Learning (RL) agents consistently find superior solutions. We further demonstrate that the control pulse envelope is a non-trivial factor that actively shapes the control landscape, which determines the difficulty for all protocols and highlights the adaptability of the RL agent. We conclude that no single strategy is universally dominant. A clear picture emerges: the future of high-fidelity quantum control lies in a synthesis of physics-informed design, as exemplified by robust hybrid methods, and the specialized, high-performance optimization power of adaptive machine learning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12832","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:49:36Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:49:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-22T02:47:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13214","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13214","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13214","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuecheng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xuecheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danlei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Danlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuheng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yuheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"End4: End-to-end Denoising Diffusion for Diffusion-Based Inpainting Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T16:19:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:55:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-18T16:03:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:48:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The powerful generative capabilities of diffusion models have significantly advanced the field of image synthesis, enhancing both full image generation and inpainting-based image editing. Despite their remarkable advancements, diffusion models also raise concerns about potential misuse for malicious purposes. However, existing approaches struggle to identify images generated by diffusion-based inpainting models, even when similar inpainted images are included in their training data. To address this challenge, we propose a novel detection method based on End-to-end denoising diffusion (End4). Specifically, End4 designs a denoising reconstruction model to improve the alignment degree between the latent spaces of the reconstruction and detection processes, thus reconstructing features that are more conducive to detection. Meanwhile, it leverages a Scale-aware Pyramid-like Fusion Module (SPFM) that refines local image features under the guidance of attention pyramid layers at different scales, enhancing feature discriminability. Additionally, to evaluate detection performance on inpainted images, we establish a comprehensive benchmark comprising images generated from five distinct masked regions. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our End4 effectively generalizes to unseen masking patterns and remains robust under various perturbations. Our code and dataset will be released soon."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13214","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:58:33Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:58:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-19T02:13:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12275","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12275","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12275","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghua","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jinghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lichun","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Lichun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenbo","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Zhenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoqin","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Haoqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Omni-CLST: Error-aware Curriculum Learning with guided Selective chain-of-Thought for audio question answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T06:54:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:01:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T03:05:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:18:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T07:19:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:25:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid progress of large audio-language models (LALMs), audio question answering (AQA) has emerged as a challenging task requiring both fine-grained audio understanding and complex reasoning. While current methods mainly rely on constructing new datasets via captioning or reasoning traces, existing high-quality AQA data remains underutilized. To address this, we propose Omni-CLST, an error-aware Curriculum Learning framework with guided Selective Chain-of-Thought. The framework efficiently leverages existing high-quality dataset through two key strategies: an error-aware curriculum that organizes samples by difficulty, and a guided thought dropout mechanism that focuses reasoning on challenging cases. Experiments show that Omni-CLST achieves 73.80% on MMAU-mini and a new state of the art of 64.30% on MMAR, demonstrating robust generalization in multimodal audio-language understanding."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 1 figure, 2 tables submitted to icassp, under prereview"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12275","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:36:43Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:36:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-19T02:11:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13240","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13240","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13240","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingyi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinchao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Don't Forget the Nonlinearity: Unlocking Activation Functions in Efficient Fine-Tuning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T16:47:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:56:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-18T04:46:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:20:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) methods primarily adapt weight matrices while keeping activation functions fixed. We introduce \\textbf{NoRA}, the first PEFT framework that directly adapts nonlinear activation functions in pretrained transformer-based models. NoRA replaces fixed activations with learnable rational functions and applies structured low-rank updates to numerator and denominator coefficients, with a group-wise design that localizes adaptation and improves stability at minimal cost. On vision transformers trained on CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100, NoRA matches or exceeds full fine-tuning while updating only 0.4\\% of parameters (0.02M), achieving accuracy gains of +0.17\\% and +0.27\\%. When combined with LoRA (\\textbf{NoRA++}), it outperforms LoRA and DoRA under matched training budgets by adding fewer trainable parameters. On LLaMA3-8B instruction tuning, NoRA++ consistently improves generation quality, yielding average MMLU gains of +0.3\\%--0.8\\%, including +1.6\\% on STEM (Alpaca) and +1.3\\% on OpenOrca. We further show that NoRA constrains adaptation to a low-dimensional functional subspace, implicitly regularizing update magnitude and direction. These results establish activation-space tuning as a complementary and highly parameter-efficient alternative to weight-based PEFT, positioning activation functions as first-class objects for model adaptation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13240","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:59:09Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:59:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-19T02:10:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":281,"totalPages":12,"page":4},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=4\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-16T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-17T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+agent+OR+agents+OR+diffusion+OR+multimodal+OR+GPU+OR+MoE+OR+%22state+space%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22vision+language%22%29","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=5\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-16T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-17T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+agent+OR+agents+OR+diffusion+OR+multimodal+OR+GPU+OR+MoE+OR+%22state+space%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22vision+language%22%29"}}